Your landlord had **30 days after you moved out** to send you a written notice saying he was claiming part or all of your deposit.

**What the 30-day rule requires:**
- The notice has to be in writing and sent by **certified mail**. Email also counts, but only if you both signed a lease addendum agreeing to email notices under Fla. Stat. § 83.505.
- **If he missed the deadline, he loses the right to keep any of the deposit.** This applies if you gave him a forwarding address.
- If he isn't claiming anything, he must return the full deposit (plus interest, if any is owed) within **15 days** of move-out.

**If he did send the notice on time:**
- You have **15 days from receiving it to object in writing**. If you don't object, the law treats that as agreeing to the deductions. So if you got a notice recently, send a written objection right away.
- He then has to pay you any remaining balance within 30 days of his notice.

**If you end up in court:** Whoever wins a deposit lawsuit gets their court costs and reasonable attorney's fees paid by the other side (§ 83.49(3)(c)). That helps you if your case is strong, but it's a risk if it's weak. Deposit disputes usually go to small claims court. The Florida Courts small claims self-help page is here: https://www.flcourts.gov/Courts-System/florida-courts-help/other-resources/small-claims

**Things to check:**
1. The date you moved out
2. Whether you gave him a forwarding address
3. Whether and when you got a certified-mail (or agreed-upon email) notice. Look at the postmark date.

**Source:** PropInspect's Florida Security Deposit guide, https://propinspect.net/security-deposit-laws/florida, last reviewed September 18, 2026. It's based on Fla. Stat. § 83.49 ([official text](https://www.leg.state.fl.us/Statutes/index.cfm?App_mode=Display_Statute&URL=0000-0099%2F0083%2FSections%2F0083.49.html)).

